<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Population Growth</title>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's begin with a simple model for how the Earth's population will
  evolve.  For a first guess, we will suppose that
  \begin{quote}
    <em>the rate of change of the population is proportional to the
      population</em>.  
  \end{quote}</p>
        <p>In mathematical notation, this assumption may be written
  <md>\frac{dP}{dt}=kP.</md>
  What is the solution to this differential equation with <m>P(0)=P_0</m>?
  (We've done this several times.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>The quantity <md>k=\frac{\frac{dP}{dt}}{P}</md> is often called the per
  capita growth rate.  It measures how much of the rate of change of
  <m>P</m> is due to a single person.</p>
        <p>Here is some data.  Use it to estimate the per capita growth
  rate.  (The population is given in billions.)</p>
        <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
      \hline
      Year &amp; Population <m>P(t)</m> 
      \hline
      1970 &amp; 3.70 
      1980 &amp; 4.44 
      1990 &amp; 5.27 
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose we say that <m>t=0</m> corresponds to the year 1980.
  What function satisfies the assumption we've made?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does this predict for the population in the year 2080?
  2180?  2580?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Does this seem like a reasonable model for population growth?
  Explain why or why not.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Our original model where <m>dP/dt = kP</m> is clearly not useful for
  making long-term predictions.  Realistically, we don't expect the
  per capita growth rate <m>k</m> to be constant.  It would probably make
  more sense to imagine that the per capita growth rate decreases when
  the population becomes very large.  In our new model, we will assume
  that the per capita growth rate is a linearly decreasing function
  <md>
  \frac{\frac{dP}{dt}}P = k(N-P).
  </md></p>
        <p>Here is some more data for the world's population.
  Complete the table by estimating the derivative
  <m>dP/dt</m> as the average rate of change of the population over 10 year
  intervals.  Then find the per capita growth rate.</p>
        <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      Year &amp; Population <m>P(t)</m> &amp; <m>dP/dt</m> &amp; <m>\frac 1PdP/dt</m> 
      \hline
      1960 &amp; 3.02\vphantom{\Huge M} &amp; --- &amp; --- 
      1970 &amp; 3.70\vphantom{\Huge M}  &amp;  &amp;  
      1980 &amp; 4.44\vphantom{\Huge M}  &amp; &amp; 
      1990 &amp; 5.27\vphantom{\Huge M}  &amp; &amp; 
      2000 &amp; 6.06\vphantom{\Huge M}  &amp; --- &amp; --- 
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
        <p>For your three data points, plot the per capita growth rate versus
  the population below:</p>
        <p>%    \epsfbox[100 400 300 550]{popgraph.eps}
    <image source="figures/popgraph.eps"/></p>
        <p>Now sketch in a straight line that seems to best fit your data and
  use this to determine the constants <m>k</m> and <m>N</m> in the logistic
  equation.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the differential equation for <m>dP/dt</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Sketch the rate of change <m>dP/dt</m> as a function of <m>P</m>.
  (Don't worry about the scale on the axes.  We're just interested in
  the shape.)</p>
        <p>For what values of the population is <m>dP/dt =0</m>?
  What does this mean?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Based upon your graph, for what values of the population
  will the population increase?  For what values will it decrease?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does this predict about the Earth's population?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
